<a name="custom"></a>
<h2>Writing a module</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A module of your own extends <tt>TModule</tt>, declares the properties the configuration
will set, and does its work in <tt>init</tt> or in a handler attached to an application
lifecycle event.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
class InventoryModule extends \Prado\TModule
{
    private $_warehouse = '';

    public function getWarehouse()
    {
        return $this-&gt;_warehouse;
    }

    public function setWarehouse($value)
    {
        $this-&gt;_warehouse = $value;
    }

    public function init($config)
    {
        parent::init($config);
        $this-&gt;getApplication()-&gt;attachEventHandler('onEndRequest', [$this, 'flush']);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;module id="inventory" class="Application.modules.InventoryModule" Warehouse="north" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
Pages and controls then reach it by id.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
$inventory = $this-&gt;getApplication()-&gt;getModule('inventory');
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
Every module, shipped or written, is configured the same way. See
<a href="?page=Configurations.AppConfig">application configurations</a> for the syntax,
and <a href="?page=Advanced.Plugins">plugins</a> for shipping one as a Composer package.
</p>

</com:TContent>
